Instruction: Read the segment of the merger agreement below and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the assessment process for a Superior Proposal.

Question: Is 'financial point of view' the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt:
SECTION 9.10 Enhanced Proposal Evaluation. Notwithstanding any provision to the contrary, during the Special Negotiation Period, the Board of Directors may determine that an Acquisition Proposal qualifies as a Superior Proposal only if, and only if, the following conditions have been met: 
   (i) Prior to any recommendation change, the Board shall obtain a comprehensive analysis from both a financial advisor and an independent legal counsel; 
   (ii) In addition, the Board must secure a detailed market trend and technological innovation report from an external consultant of recognized industry expertise to assess the strategic alignment of the Proposal with long-term corporate growth; 
   (iii) The Board is required to engage in a minimum of a five Business Day negotiation period with the existing Parent, during which proposed adjustments to not only the pricing and financing structures but also to the operational and strategic commitments of the Proposal are to be evaluated; 
   (iv) Only after integrating insights from the financial analysis, legal due diligence, and the market-technology report—including forecasts of industry shifts and potential synergy benefits—can the Board declare a Proposal as Superior. 
Furthermore, the Agreement explicitly states that while financial metrics are critical, they are not considered in isolation from the broader strategic and legal factors relevant to the business’s fiduciary duties. 


